    "difficulty": "Challenging",
    "isSketch": false,
    "statement": "In an infinite two-dimensional grid, the number in each box is greater than or equal to the average of the numbers in its four neighbouring boxes. Show that all the numbers in the grid are the same (assuming the values are bounded or attain a maximum).",
    "hints": [
      "Let $M$ be the maximum value in the grid, attained at some cell $(i_0, j_0)$.",
      "Write down the inequality for $(i_0, j_0)$: $M \\ge \\frac{1}{4}(u_{\\text{N}} + u_{\\text{S}} + u_{\\text{E}} + u_{\\text{W}})$, knowing each neighbour is $\\le M$.",
      "Can any of the four neighbours be strictly less than $M$? Now propagate this argument across the entire grid by induction!"
    ],
    "solution": "Let $u(i,j)$ be the value at $(i,j)$. Suppose $u$ attains a global maximum $M$ at some cell $(i_0, j_0)$. By hypothesis:\n$$M = u(i_0, j_0) \\ge \\frac{u(i_0+1, j_0) + u(i_0-1, j_0) + u(i_0, j_0+1) + u(i_0, j_0-1)}{4}.$$\nSince every neighbour satisfies $u(x,y) \\le M$, if any single neighbour were strictly less than $M$, their average would be strictly less than $M$, a contradiction. Therefore, all four neighbours must equal $M$.\nBy induction on Manhattan distance from $(i_0, j_0)$, every cell in the infinite connected grid equals $M$. (This is the discrete Maximum Principle for subharmonic functions; note that if values are only bounded above and not assumed to attain a maximum, on $\\mathbb{Z}^2$ a bounded subharmonic function is still constant, though the interview prompt explicitly simplifies by assuming a maximum is attained or boundedness.)",
    "number": 17
