    "solution": "Substitute $u = 1/x$, so $x = 1/u$ and $dx = -\\frac{1}{u^2}\\,du$. As $x$ goes from $1$ to $\\infty$, $u$ goes from $1$ to $0^+$:\n$$\\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{x}\\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)dx = \\int_1^0 u \\sin(u) \\left(-\\frac{1}{u^2}\\,du\\right) = \\int_0^1 \\frac{\\sin u}{u}\\,du.$$\nSince $\\lim_{u\\to 0} \\frac{\\sin u}{u} = 1$, the integrand has only a removable discontinuity at $u=0$ and is bounded ($|\\frac{\\sin u}{u}| \\le 1$) and continuous on $[0, 1]$. Therefore the integral **converges** to a finite value (roughly $0.946$). *(Alternatively, for $x \\ge 1$, $0 \\le \\frac{1}{x}\\sin(1/x) \\le \\frac{1}{x^2}$, and $\\int_1^\\infty \\frac{dx}{x^2} = 1$ converges by comparison!)*",
